# The Future of Civil Resistance

**Authors:** Erica Chenoweth

## Abstract
<p>This chapter discusses the long-term effects of civil resistance on different societies, noting
                        whether successful civil resistance campaigns tend to leave societies better or worse off. It
                        explores how civil resistance campaigns have changed over the past decade, particularly with the
                        emergence of digital activism. It also considers how movements have persisted in the face of
                        rising authoritarianism, global economic crises, and the global coronavirus pandemic. The
                        chapter points out that more people today turn to nonviolent civil resistance than to violence
                        as a way to remedy dire circumstances, which has become increasingly true over the past fifty
                        years. According to comprehensive data on revolutionary campaigns-nonviolent and violent- 627
                        mass campaigns sought to overthrow dictatorships or achieve independence from 1900 to 2019.</p>

**Source:** https://doi.org/10.1093/wentk/9780190244392.003.0006

